V9 dynamic VRAM-pressure handling (Qwen3.5-4B-Q8, RTX 5050 8 GB laptop, idle desktop)

GATES (set before implementation)
 C1  existing tests unchanged; greedy chat/server output byte-identical to the previous path; pinned HF verify passes
 C2  a mark kept in host memory restores the exact bytes of a device mark (bit-identical logits afterwards)
 R1  chat/code: with free VRAM anywhere above what one pass needs, every turn reads only the new tokens
     (replayed 0) and its wall time stays within 10% of the unpressured turn at the same context
 R2  server session: a turn under pressure costs within 10% of the unpressured turn (no history replay
     after the response); uncontended server peak VRAM does not rise
 U1  uncontended: decode tok/s, TTFT and chat turn wall within 3% (interleaved A/B on the same machine)
 F1  injected failures after partial writes: a host mark that cannot be put back leaves a cache that is
     cleared for replay, never a partly restored one; a refused mark falls back to a reported replay
 S1  concurrent requests under pressure: no HTTP 500 from a GPU allocation failure that waiting for another
     request would cure; anything refused is reported as retryable
 M1  host memory use is bounded: at most one recurrent-state mark (about 53 MB) per conversation
 O1  a replay that pressure causes is reported without prompt text (chat `replayed`, server `marin_replayed_tokens`)

HARNESS (19-harness/): chat_pressure.sh + long_chat.rs.txt (a chat of ~700-token turns; a second program takes GPU memory
after a chosen turn and leaves a chosen amount free), srv_sessions.py (server turns / concurrent requests, same ballast),
ballast.rs.txt. "free now" below is device-wide free memory after the ballast; the ballast's own CUDA context takes ~150 MiB.
Interleaved A/B: before = HEAD f3bdb3c built in a separate directory, after = this tree.

WHY MARKS WERE REFUSED (profile before the change)
 Engine::mark refused unless free >= mark (53 MiB) + the 512 MiB safety slack = ~565 MiB, although the mark itself is
 53 MiB. Engine::session_checkpoint (server) needed a whole copy of the history KV + 512 MiB (about 410 MiB at 5k tokens).
 A refused mark meant the next turn read the whole history again (chat: `replayed`; server: a full re-read after the
 response, reported as `reused`).

RECLAIMABLE MEMORY (sizes; nothing active or committed is ever evicted)
 chat / code     : nothing besides the mark: live KV and 53 MB state are the conversation itself
 server idle     : the one retained session (history KV + 53 MB) is already dropped when another request starts
                   (Store::evict); recoverable from the client's messages (404 -> resend)
 server per turn: the checkpoint copy of the history KV (was +352 MiB at 5k tokens): no longer made
 shared prefix   : 83 MiB (31 tokens, built in 209 ms): droppable but it would only move the failure point by 83 MiB
                   inside a band of ~100 MiB; not built
 workspaces      : activations are allocated per pass and freed after it; nothing persistent to reclaim

CHAT UNDER PRESSURE (ctx 8192, ~700-token turns, 4B-Q8; ballast after turn 3; wall s (tokens replayed), turns 4 / 5 / 6 / 7)
 free now   before                                          after
 ~600 MiB   6.2 (0) 6.4 (0) 35.5 (3563) 42.4 (4272)        6.2 (0) 6.4 (0) 6.7 (0) 7.0 (0)
 ~340 MiB   6.3 (0) 29.2 (2854) 35.6 (3563) 42.3 (4272)     6.4 (0) 6.5 (0) 6.7 (0) 7.1 (0)
 ~145 MiB   6.2 (0) 29.2 (2854) then a KV error             6.3 (0) 6.4 (0) 7.0 (0) then a KV error at turn 7
 (unpressured turns 4-7 of the same chat: 6.3 6.4 6.8 7.0)
 Long chat (ctx 16384, ballast at 8.5k tokens, ~340 MiB free), turns at 10.0k / 10.8k tokens:
   before 100.0 s / 109.3 s (replayed 9,325 / 10,064)   after 9.8 s / 10.0 s (replayed 0)
 Floor: with ~50-145 MiB free the next KV slab (32 MiB) cannot be allocated; the turn fails with a clear error and hint, the
 cache is as it was (the prompt slices already read stay), and the same turn after the memory came back ran in 3.1 s,
 reused 2,526 tokens, replayed 0.

SERVER SESSION UNDER PRESSURE (ctx 8192, one session, six ~830-token turns, ballast after turn 3)
 free now  turn 4 / 5 / 6 wall        before                 after
 ~585 MiB  34.3 / 42.1 / 50.4 s -> 7.4 / 7.7 / 8.1 s
 ~178 MiB  34.4 / 42.1 / 50.6 s -> 7.4 / 7.8 / 8.1 s
 Uncontended (interleaved, order swapped to cancel GPU warming): turns 6.4-8.2 s before and after; server process VRAM
 peak 5,176 -> 4,824 MiB at 5k tokens (-352 MiB: the history copy is gone).

CONCURRENT REQUESTS UNDER PRESSURE (two ~2.7k-token requests, ctx 8192)
 free now   before                                   after
 >800 MiB   both 200 (44 s)                          same
 ~400 MiB   both 500 after 32 s (KV slab OOM)        one 200 (34 s), the other 503 (retryable) after 33 s
 ~185 MiB   both 500                                 both 503 at once (one request would not fit either)
 Two ~1.7k-token decoders with 500 new tokens each: 220 MiB free -> one 200, one 503; unpressured: both 200.
 Two persistent sessions ping-pong (server keeps ONE idle session; the other gets 404 "evicted"): unchanged design.

CORRECTNESS
 Host mark returns bit-identical bytes and logits to a device mark; a host mark that cannot be put back leaves the cache
 cleared for replay, never partly restored (gpu_mark_tests). A pass that fails for memory is rolled back exactly (V4 tests).
 chat_pressure.rs and server_pressure.rs fail without the host mark (replayed 187 / 281) and pass with it; the server test
 also compares the answers of a pressured and an unpressured conversation: equal. gate tests cover the wait / give-way rule.
 Uncontended decode (marin-run bench 128/64, interleaved, order swapped): 39.92 / 39.84 vs 40.04 / 39.90 tok/s, first token 948-950 vs 950-964 ms.

LIMITS
 - The younger of two requests that both run out of memory is refused (503), not parked: parking would mean dropping its
   cache and reading it again later. The client may retry.
 - A batched decode step that fails still marks every cache for replay (V7); under pressure it was not seen to matter
   (no run showed a replay penalty) but it is not rolled back either.
 - Below ~150 MiB free a single pass cannot run; the planner's 512 MiB slack is unchanged.
 - The server still keeps one idle session; the shared prefix (83 MiB) is a full copy.
